Adichanallūr, prehistoric cemetery at, 97 ;
its urns and tombs of Stone Age and of
much more recent times, 97.

Akbar, some references to: his patronage
of foreign artists, 127, 129, 130, 199;
his failure to found a national school,
131; his coinage and its ambiguous
legend, 146; his seventy mints, 146;
his tomb at Sikandra, 127.

Bājī Rao II, seventh and last Peshwā
(1795–1818), his disputed supremacy,
443 ; flight to British protection, 443 ;
accepted (1802) Treaty of Bassein, 443 ;
reinstated after third Marāthā War,
443 ; intrigues with chiefs against
British, 444 ; attack on the Resident
at Poona, defeat in fourth War, 444 ;
deposition and confiscation of his dominions, 444 ; died (1853) a pensioner,
444 ; Nāna Sāhib his adopted son, 444.

Crystal, only one record inscribed on, 36;
crystal phials and caskets, associated
with or containing records, 36, 37;
description of crystal bowl at Piprahwa,
133.

Delhi, Prithwī Rāj its last (1192) Hindu
king, 353, 354; thirty-four Muhammadan kings of (1206–1526), 357–368;
their five houses, 357; successions generally contested, 357; tables of, 368, 369.

East India Company, coinage of, 148, 458;
Shāhjahān’s the basis of it and of the
present British coinage, 145, 146.

Forde, Colonel, sent by Clive (1759)
from Bengal to Northern Circārs, 473;
defeated Conflans at Condore and captured (1760) Masulipatam, 473.

Forgeries, numismatic and literary, 65;
of inscriptions, 65, 66; reference to lists
of known, 66.

Horse-sacrifice (Aśvamēdha), only by a
king with undisputed claims to universal dominion, 38, 291; in the Rigveda, 221; by Samudragupta, 38, 291;
by the Rāthor of Kanauj, 314.

Iron Age, the, of primitive man, 89, 91;
a knowledge of iron probably brought
to Northern India from Babylonia, much
later to Southern India from Egypt, 98;
its megalithic tombs, 95, 96.

Jahāngīr, fourth Mughal emperor of India
(1605-27), eldest son and successor of
Akbar, 399; his moderate success in
the Deccan, 399, 400; his expeditions
to Kābul, 399, 400; saved from his chief
general by his wife, Nūr Jahān, 400;
the rebellion of his eldest son, 400; received Sir Thomas Roe at Ajmer, 400;
the chief buildings in his reign, 400;
his patronage of Florentine artists, 127;
his coinage, with his portrait, wine cup
in hand, 146, 147; his buildings, 199,
200; his tomb at Lahore, 128.

Mahāvastu, the only complete canonical
work of the Hīnayāna in Sanskrit, 260;
Tibetan and Chinese translations, 260.

North-West Frontier of India, 521, 522;
the Durand agreement (1893) with
the Amīr of Afghānistān, 524, 525;
strengthened by Lord Roberts, 522;
the compromise (1899) between the
Lawrence and Forward policies, 526,
526. See also under Russia.

Begam for aiding rebellion, 484; cession of the Doāb to Wellesley, 489;
annexed (1856) for misgovernment by
Lord Dalhousie, 507.

Prākrit, its relation to Sanskrit and Pāli,
208; its use in the Sanskrit drama, 245;
the language of Jainism, 208, 261, and
of old Buddhism, 208, 259; its secular
literature, folk-lore, 267; its lyrics, 267;
its drama, 267; its kāvyas, 267, 268.

Shāhu, grandson and heir of Sivājī, 440;
his long nominal reign (1707–48), 440;
the transfer of power to his minister,
the Peshwā, 441.

Siddharājā, famous Rājput king and magician (1093–1143) of Gujarāt, 313;
carried about the Ponwār king in a
cage, 313.

Silver: of early coinage, most probably
imported then as now, 136; brass substituted for, in the Tughlak coins, 145;
not mentioned in the Rigveda, 321.

Vikramāditya VI, of the Western Chālukyas (1070-1126), defeated and deposed
his brother, 337; his long and generally
peaceful reign, 337; his liberal patronage of learning and Buddhism, 337;
the rise of the Hoysalas, 337, 338;
